Let $I$ be an infinite set and let $D$ be a regular ultrafilter on $I$. (Recall that $D$ is regular if there exists a family $\{X_i : i \in I\} \subseteq D$ such that every infinite subfamily has empty intersection.) Consider the linear order $(\omega,<)^I/D$, the reduced power of the natural numbers modulo $D$.

For a pair of regular cardinals $(\kappa,\mu)$ with $\kappa,\mu \le |I|$, say that $(\kappa,\mu)$ is a cut of $(\omega,<)^I/D$ if there exist sequences $(a_\alpha)_{\alpha<\kappa}$ and $(b_\beta)_{\beta<\mu}$ in $(\omega,<)^I/D$ such that:
(i) $a_\alpha < a_{\alpha'}$ whenever $\alpha < \alpha' < \kappa$;
(ii) $b_{\beta'} < b_\beta$ whenever $\beta < \beta' < \mu$;
(iii) $a_\alpha < b_\beta$ for all $\alpha<\kappa$, $\beta<\mu$;
(iv) there is no $c$ in $(\omega,<)^I/D$ satisfying $a_\alpha < c < b_\beta$ for all $\alpha,\beta$.
The cut spectrum $C(D)$ is the set of all such pairs $(\kappa,\mu)$. A cut $(\kappa,\mu)$ is symmetric if $\kappa = \mu$, and asymmetric otherwise.

Assume $C(D) \neq \emptyset$, and let $\kappa$ be the least regular cardinal $\le |I|$ for which the symmetric cut $(\kappa,\kappa)$ lies in $C(D)$. Determine the set of regular cardinals $\mu < \kappa$ for which the asymmetric cut $(\kappa,\mu)$ can also belong to $C(D)$.

